(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 11, part: `Part Four: The Chair`, title: `Go-Live`, weeks: `Months 13 to 15`,
  tasks: [`SC3`,`SC5`,`SC6`,`SD4`,`C3`,`C5`,`E5`,`E2`,`L20`,`L15`,`L6`,`L5`,`L14`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Friday, October 8, at seven in the morning, the go or no-go meeting for One Chart took nine minutes.`,
    `Maria had built it to be short. One page on the screen in the big room on the second floor: the exit criteria everyone had signed in August, with a column for met or not met. Testing. Contract acceptance tests, 7.3 included. The Rawlins volume test, rerun. Downtime procedures, practiced on paper on every Wyoming unit the Saturday before, with the plug pulled. Training, ninety-six percent of Wyoming users through their courses and a proficiency check, and no login for anyone without one. She read each line out loud. Its owner said met and signed. Gail signed for Rawlins last, the way she'd asked to, and capped the pen like she was closing a med drawer.`,
    `Ochoa had sat along the wall the whole time. On the way out he stopped at her chair. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Cutover starts at midnight a week from tonight. I'll be in your command center in Cheyenne all weekend,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Dry, plain, a little amused.">"As furniture."</span>`,
    `The command center roster was the last page in her folder. She'd written it three times. Days, seven to seven: Maria Santos. Nights, seven to seven: Kiran Bhatt.`,
    `Kiran read it standing by the coffee urn. He'd been back for five weeks, home by five most days, and the analyst Marcus had been teaching now built medications beside him without asking him first. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"In September you texted me at nine at night and asked who my second was,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"This is my answer."</span> He read it again. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Twelve hours."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Twelve hours. Then you go home and you don't answer your phone. Same for me. Command center rule."</span> He pinned it to the wall of his cubicle, beside the monitor, where he'd see it at seven.`,
    `Her phone buzzed on the way back to her office. Lucia. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"8 days."</span> Maria typed back the first date she'd ever set for it herself. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Saturday the 23rd. I'll pick you up."</span> The answer came before she reached her desk. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"That's a date."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: go-live, and after`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Four ways to teach`,
        body: [`The Sunday before cutover, Ochoa took the back booth at the Greeley diner and wrote four words down the legal pad. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You'll be asked which one,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The answer is almost always more than one."</span>`,
          `Computer-based learning, CBL, is self-paced e-learning. It's cheap to repeat and fits people whose schedules won't fit a class, like night staff and traveling doctors. Classroom training is led by an instructor, with hands-on practice in a training system on realistic test patients. It fits complex workflows and people with questions. Train the trainer teaches a small group deeply, and they teach their peers, which scales and leaves the skill behind after the vendor leaves. At-the-elbow support puts superusers beside clinicians on the floor during go-live, answering questions at the moment of work.`,
          `Train by role, close to go-live, on the scenarios people will actually face, because adults learn what they'll use tomorrow. Check proficiency before anyone gets a login. Superusers come from the floor, get extra training, carry no patients during go-live, and cover every shift, nights included, sized by census and need.`],
        terms: [[`Computer-based learning`,`Self-paced e-learning, often role-based, that people take on their own schedule.`],[`Train the trainer`,`Training a small group deeply so they can teach and support their peers.`],[`At-the-elbow support`,`Superusers working beside clinicians on the units during go-live, helping in the moment.`]],
        exam: `Match the method to the need. Basics for many people on mixed schedules point to CBL. Complex workflows point to classroom practice. Scaling and sustaining point to train the trainer. Help in the moment of care is at-the-elbow. Access waits on a proficiency check.` },
      { h: `The people side`,
        body: [`Ochoa drew a staircase. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The system goes live in one night. People go live one at a time."</span> Organizational change management works on the human side of a change. One common model, ADKAR, names five steps: awareness of why, desire to take part, knowledge of how, ability to do it in real work, and reinforcement so it sticks. Treat resistance as information, and ask what it protects before you push on it. Champions, respected peers who use the system well, persuade better than any memo, and the best ones were skeptics first. Reinforce by measuring adoption, sharing it back, and fixing what people say is in the way.`,
          `Clinical systems is also an internal consultant. When a department asks for help, find the need behind the request, bring data and options with their costs, recommend one, and let the owner decide. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You're not the order taker, and you don't run their unit,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"You're the one who knows what each choice costs."</span>`],
        terms: [[`ADKAR`,`A change model: awareness, desire, knowledge, ability, reinforcement.`],[`Change champion`,`A respected peer who models the new way of working and helps others adopt it.`],[`Consultative services`,`Helping a department decide: diagnose the need, present options and tradeoffs, recommend, and let the owner choose.`]],
        exam: `For resistance after go-live, pick the answer that listens, fixes the real barrier, uses peer champions, and reinforces with measures before any mandate. For a department's request, the consultative answer diagnoses, offers options, recommends, and leaves the decision with the owner.` },
      { h: `The command center`,
        body: [`A go-live runs from a command center: one place where every issue is logged, given a severity and an owner, and tracked until it's closed. Hypercare is the weeks of extra support after go-live, until the numbers settle. Hold huddles at shift change, and publish a few key performance indicators, KPIs, each with a target: open issues by severity and age, time to resolve, tickets per 100 users, and first-contact resolution.`,
          `Tickets per 100 users is tickets divided by users, times 100, so sites of different sizes compare fairly. First-contact resolution is tickets resolved on the first contact divided by all tickets. A low rate usually means questions the desk can't answer, which is often a training gap. Read the trend by site, shift, and workflow, the way Tasha does. An incident is one interruption, and the job is to restore service. A problem is the cause behind repeated incidents, found by trending them and finding the root cause.`,
          `A service level agreement turns a vendor's promises into measures. Measure them with your own data, agree when the clock starts and stops, use the remedies the contract gives you, and review them at a regular meeting with the vendor.`],
        terms: [[`Hypercare`,`The period of extra support right after go-live, until issue volumes and key measures settle.`],[`Tickets per 100 users`,`Tickets divided by users, times 100. Lets you compare support load across sites of different sizes.`],[`First-contact resolution`,`Tickets resolved on the first contact divided by all tickets.`]],
        exam: `Expect the math, and expect to read a trend. Many how-do-I tickets about one workflow point to targeted training. An SLA is judged with the organization's own measurements and the contract's definitions.` },
      { h: `Keeping it running`,
        body: [`After go-live the system has to be maintained: patches, fixes, upgrades, and the content inside it. Every upgrade starts with the vendor's release notes, read against your own build to find what changes for you. Then test it in a copy of production, with full regression on the workflows that matter most, edge cases included. Schedule it with the people who'll live through it, run downtime procedures during the window, keep a back-out plan, and tell users what changed. Don't fall behind. A system that skips upgrades stops getting fixes, which is how MedaLink got here.`],
        terms: [[`Release notes`,`The vendor's list of what an upgrade changes, read against the local build to plan testing.`]],
        exam: `For an upgrade, the best answer reads the release notes, tests in a non-production copy with regression, schedules downtime with users, and has a back-out plan. Skipping upgrades isn't the safe choice.` },
      { h: `Did it work`,
        body: [`Ochoa turned to a clean page. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Your business case made promises. Now you find out which ones came true."</span> Benefits realization compares each measure with the baseline taken before go-live, at the dates the plan set, reported by the owner it names. Percent change is the new value minus the baseline, divided by the baseline. A move from 80 to 90 percent is ten percentage points and a 12.5 percent change. Say which one you mean. Report return on investment and peer benchmarks only when the numbers are in.`,
          `Read outcomes over time. Documentation time often rises after go-live and falls as people learn, so plot every week against the baseline and don't call it in week one or week four. Credit other changes their share. Measure effectiveness, like BCMA scan compliance and turnaround time, and satisfaction, with user surveys against their own baseline.`,
          `Look past your walls, too. Receiving hospitals, the HIE, and home health need to know a feed changed, and you verify they receive it. Judge patient tools by use: a portal counts when patients open their results. Ochoa tore the page off. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Go-live is the day you find out what you built. Benefits are the year you find out if it mattered."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Percent change`,`(New value minus baseline) divided by baseline.`],[`Percentage points`,`The plain difference between two percentages, like 80 to 90 percent.`],[`Learning curve`,`The rise and fall in effort, like documentation time, as people get used to a new system.`]],
        exam: `Expect percent change from a baseline, and the percentage point trap. Benefits are measured against a pre-go-live baseline and reported by their owners. A portal is judged by use. A feed change isn't done until outside partners confirm they receive it.` }
    ],
    video: [`fY4U_GEFd5A`,`Super Users, UAT and Training | The Trio Behind Successful Health IT Go-Lives`,`Draegan Network`,`6:32`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f11s1`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L15`, title:`Locums`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, October 11, Gail called with a problem nobody had put on a page. The Rawlins emergency department ran on traveling doctors, locums, who flew in for a week of nights and flew out. Twelve were on the schedule for the six weeks after go-live. Seven had never used Corvane, and none of them could come to Wyoming early for the eight-hour physician class.`,
        `Corvane's training lead had an answer ready. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Give them a tip sheet and a login. They're doctors. They'll figure it out."</span>`,
        (G.sharedlogin
          ?`Gail had heard one more idea, and she didn't like it. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"My ED charge nurse asked if the locums could use a department login the first night, like in September."</span> She let that sit. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"I told her I'd ask you. I already know the answer."</span>`
          :`Gail had already decided what she wouldn't do. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Nobody works my ED on somebody else's login. Not after September. So tell me how these doctors learn it."</span>`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Give each locum the tip sheet and a login on arrival, with the help desk number.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The tip sheet is good, and it's two pages long. The first locum spends his first hour on the phone with the help desk while the waiting room fills.`,
          why:`A tip sheet is a reference, not training. Clinicians need practice on realistic work, and a check that they can do it, before they care for patients in a new system.`},
        {t:`Assign the physician e-learning before each first shift, and grant access after it.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The e-learning gets done, mostly in airports. Nobody checks whether it stuck. On the first Saturday a locum orders a drip in the wrong units, and pharmacy catches it at 2 AM.`,
          why:`E-learning is the right method for people who can't attend a class. Finishing it isn't the same as being able to do it, though. Check proficiency before access, and put a superuser beside them on the first shift.`},
        {t:`Use role-based e-learning, then a proficiency check, then a superuser on shift one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Kiran's team cuts the physician course down to what an ED doctor does at night: the track board, orders, the sepsis pop-up, a transfer out, and signing a note. Ninety minutes, online, with test patients. Each locum takes it before flying in, then sits with a superuser for a twenty-minute proficiency check, five tasks done without help. No pass, no login. On each locum's first shift, a superuser stays at the elbow until midnight. The first one, a doctor from Tennessee, passes on her second try and says she wishes every hospital did it this way. Gail writes the plan on her whiteboard under one word. Doctors.`,
          why:`Pick the method for the learner and the work. Computer-based learning reaches people whose schedules won't fit a class, especially when it's built by role on real scenarios. A proficiency check proves the skill before access, and at-the-elbow support covers the first real shift. Good plans blend methods.`},
        {t:`Require every locum to take the eight-hour class before working a single shift.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Three locums say no, and the staffing agency can't replace them in time. Rawlins goes into go-live with two nights a week uncovered in the ED.`,
          why:`Classroom training is valuable, but a class nobody can attend trains nobody. Match the method to the learner's schedule, and don't trade training for coverage.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s2`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC3`, title:`Nights`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, October 12, Corvane's go-live support plan came over. One Corvane trainer for every ten users on day shift, at all three hospitals, and a phone number for nights. Rawlins would have two trainers on days, and the phone after seven.`,
        `Maria had sized the plan from census numbers back in February. Rawlins averaged fourteen inpatients plus whoever was in the ED, and its nights had one doctor and three nurses. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Nights are where my trouble lives,"</span> Gail said on the call. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"A phone number at three in the morning is the help desk with a nicer name."</span>`,
        `Twenty minutes later an email came in from 4 West at Greeley, forwarded by the nurse manager. Brooke had been a sepsis alert superuser since the spring, and she was asking to go to Rawlins for the first two weeks. Nights, she'd written, if anybody needed nights.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Staff superusers on every shift, nights too, sized by census, with no patients of their own.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`By Thursday it's one page. Superusers on every unit, every shift, for two weeks, then tapering as the ticket numbers fall. Rawlins gets one at the elbow on each unit at night and a second in the ED. Each superuser has a vest, no patient assignment, and a way to log every question to the command center. Corvane's trainers fill in on days. And twelve Wyoming nurses, two from each shift at each hospital, spent the summer in train the trainer sessions, so the skill stays when the vests go home. Brooke gets nights on the Rawlins medical floor. She answers the schedule with one line. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Quiet, sure.">"Good. Nights are when nobody's there."</span>`,
          why:`At-the-elbow support works when it's where the work is, on every shift, sized to the census and the need. Superusers from the floor, freed from patient assignments, can teach at the moment it matters. Train the trainer leaves the skill behind after the vendor goes home.`},
        {t:`Keep Corvane's trainers on days, and have a superuser on call from home each night.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's better than a phone number. At 3 AM the superuser on call is twenty minutes away and asleep, and the nurse with the question has a patient who can't wait twenty minutes.`,
          why:`Covering nights is right, and on call beats a phone line. At-the-elbow means beside the clinician when the question comes up, not on the way.`},
        {t:`Have each unit's charge nurse act as superuser, on top of running the unit that shift.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The charge nurses try. The first night, a Laramie charge nurse is showing a new nurse the sepsis order set while two admissions arrive at once, and both jobs get half of her.`,
          why:`A superuser carrying another full job helps nobody well. Free superusers from other duties for the go-live.`},
        {t:`Use Corvane's plan as written, since its trainers know the system better than anyone.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Corvane's trainers know Corvane. They don't know Rawlins, and at night there aren't any. The phone number rings at a desk in another time zone.`,
          why:`Vendor trainers help, but they don't know your units, and nights matter as much as days. Size support to your census and cover every shift.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s3`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L5`, title:`The command center`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, October 16, MedaLink went read-only at midnight, and for six hours the three Wyoming hospitals ran on paper while the conversion loaded. Corvane came up at 6:04 AM. Maria watched it from a conference room on the second floor of the Cheyenne hospital, with eight phones, two projectors, and a whiteboard ruled into columns.`,
        `By nine the phones were ringing in waves. A printer in Laramie wouldn't print labels. A Rawlins nurse couldn't find a patient who'd been admitted on paper at 3 AM. A Cheyenne doctor wanted his old order set. Every call was real, and every one sounded like the most important thing in the building.`,
        ...(G.skippedregression?[`At 10:40 one call was worse than the rest. Discharge medication lists at Cheyenne were printing without their last page. It was a defect in a workflow covered only by the 126 regression scripts cut in August, and nobody had run them since. Kiran found it in twenty minutes. It had been sitting in the build for a month.`]:[]),
        `Ochoa sat at the far end of the table with a coffee and no laptop. Twice he started to stand when the phones lit up. Twice he sat back down. The second time, the call was the Cheyenne hospital president, asking for him by name. Ochoa pointed down the table at Maria.`,
        `Late that morning, Corvane's project director asked what Maria wanted on the board for the noon huddle. His tool could report ninety-one measures.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`All ninety-one measures from Corvane's tool, so nobody can say anything was hidden.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Ninety-one numbers go up on two projectors, and nobody reads them. At the noon huddle the room argues about which ones matter while the phones keep ringing.`,
          why:`More measures isn't more insight. Pick the few that tell you whether care and support are working, each with a target.`},
        {t:`Ticket counts by hospital, updated every hour, so everyone sees where calls come from.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The counts are useful, and they're raw. Cheyenne has the most calls because it has the most people. The board can't show that Rawlins, with the fewest, is having the hardest day.`,
          why:`Counts by site are a start. Without adjusting for size and adding severity, age, and resolution, they point at the biggest site, not the one in trouble.`},
        {t:`No measures this week. Fix the calls as they come in, and count it all afterward.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The calls get fixed one at a time. By Monday nobody can say whether things are getting better, and the hospital presidents start calling Ochoa instead of her.`,
          why:`If you don't measure from the first hour, you can't tell recovery from drift. Track a few KPIs from the start.`},
        {t:`A few KPIs with targets: open issues by age, tickets per 100 users, first-call fixes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Six lines on the whiteboard, each with a target, emailed to every unit at noon and at six. Open issues by severity, and the age of the oldest. Severity ones resolved within an hour. Tickets per 100 users at each hospital. First-contact resolution at the desk. And two clinical lines Maria adds herself: doses scanned, and outside transfer med lists that arrived complete. At the noon huddle the Rawlins line is the worst, and the oldest open issue is the Laramie printer. Both have owners by 12:10. At seven Kiran comes in with a sandwich, reads the board top to bottom, and writes his name under hers. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Hotel,"</span> he says. She goes.`,
          why:`A command center needs a few key performance indicators with targets, by site, read at every huddle and shared with the floor. Adjust for size, like tickets per 100 users, so sites compare fairly, and track the clinical measures the go-live was meant to protect.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s4`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E2`, title:`Outside the walls`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Kiran's night report on Sunday morning had forty-one lines. Line twenty-three said: Rawlins, 3:10 AM, sepsis pop-up, bed six, order set started 3:14, superuser at the elbow, Brooke.`,
        `Maria called her at noon. Brooke sounded like someone who'd slept four hours and didn't mind. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Quiet, tired, a little proud.">"The nurse went to click it away. She was busy. I put my hand next to the mouse and said read it all the way down."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Quiet, plain.">"I didn't, once."</span> The patient, a woman with a kidney infection, had antibiotics running by 3:40.${G.allalerts?` Brooke had one more thing to say. On 4 West that spring she'd had six kinds of pop-up a night, most of them owned by nobody, and she'd learned to click through all of them. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"That's the habit I was watching for,"</span> she said.`:``}`,
        `Monday, October 18, at 6:10 AM, a Rawlins man with twenty-six active medications went to Greeley by ambulance. Nothing was faxed, and nothing went through Tributary. Nothing had to. The Greeley nurse opened the same chart the Rawlins nurse had closed, with all twenty-six in it and the last dose of each. Maria read the count off the command center screen and kept it to herself. There wasn't a page two. There wasn't a page. There was one chart.`,
        `At 9:30 a nurse from a hospital in Casper called the Rawlins ED. Casper wasn't part of Cascade. A Rawlins patient had gone there overnight for a heart catheterization, and the transfer document Casper always pulled from the regional HIE wasn't there. The newest one for any Rawlins patient was dated Friday. Marcus had the cause in fifteen minutes. The HIE had moved Rawlins to the new Corvane feed on Saturday as planned. Casper's system, and maybe others outside Cascade, still asked for documents at the old MedaLink address.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Rawlins fax every transfer summary to outside hospitals until the HIE sorts it out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Rawlins faxes twelve summaries that week. One reaches a clinic in Saratoga a page short, and nothing on it says so.`,
          why:`A fallback is reasonable, and fax is how page two got lost in the first place. Use a secure electronic fallback with a completeness check, and fix the feed for everyone.`},
        {t:`Have the HIE list every outside subscriber, then call each one and confirm receipt.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`By noon the HIE has the list: nine outside organizations still asking at the old address. Two hospitals, five clinics, a home health agency, and the state immunization registry. Marcus and the HIE's engineer repoint them one by one, and Maria's team calls each organization to confirm that a test document arrives, then a real one. Until each partner confirms, Rawlins sends it summaries through the HIE's secure direct messaging, with a phone call to check the medication count. Casper confirms at 2:15. The Rawlins home health agency confirms Tuesday, and its director says nobody from Cascade has ever called her before about anything.`,
          why:`A go-live changes data flows that outside organizations depend on: receiving hospitals, clinics, home health, the HIE, and public health. Find every one, tell them, verify they receive, and protect continuity of care with a safe fallback until they do.`},
        {t:`Fix Casper's connection today with the HIE, since Casper is the one who called us first.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Casper is fixed by noon. On Wednesday a clinic in Saratoga calls about the same thing. Then the home health agency.`,
          why:`Fixing the caller's problem is right. When one outside partner is broken, others usually are too, and most of them haven't noticed yet. Find them all.`},
        {t:`Tell Casper's nurse to phone the Rawlins ED for records, since the HIE owns the feed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Casper's nurse calls the Rawlins ED, which is busy, and gets the medication list read to her over the phone twenty minutes later.`,
          why:`Continuity of care across organizations is partly yours. The sending hospital and the HIE share the job. Don't push it onto the receiving nurse.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s5`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L20`, title:`The vest`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, October 19, Renee called from Laramie at seven. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Two of my surgeons are writing paper post-op orders again,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Pat's typing them in. Third day. The others are watching to see if it works."</span>`,
        `Maria drove up Highway 287 that morning and stopped at the door of the surgeons' lounge. Walt Hagen sat at the table in a lime green vest that said SUPERUSER, ASK ME across the back, with a clicker on a lanyard and a stack of order sets marked in red. He'd taken the train the trainer course in August, Renee said, and told nobody until he showed up in the vest.`,
        (G.dismissedwalt
          ?`He looked at her over his glasses. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, cool.">"Before you ask, I'm doing this for the surgeons,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Somebody has to check."</span>`
          :`He looked at her over his glasses. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, deadpan.">"Don't say anything about the vest."</span>`),
        `The two surgeons on paper had the same complaint. The post-op order set took thirty-four clicks, and paper took forty seconds. Walt had already counted. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"They're not wrong,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"They're early."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fix the order set with Walt, have him round with the two, and post CPOE rates daily.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Walt and Kiran's junior analyst spend Tuesday afternoon on the post-op set. The most common choices come up already checked, two screens become one, and it drops to twelve clicks. Walt tests it with his clicker and writes the number on the lounge whiteboard. Wednesday he rounds with the two surgeons, at the elbow, the way the vest says. By Friday one of them enters every order. The other enters most of them and complains about all of them, which Walt says is how you know it's working. Renee posts the unit's CPOE rate on the break room door every morning: 61 percent Tuesday, 88 Friday.`,
          why:`Change management treats resistance as information. Find the real barrier and fix it, put a respected peer at the elbow as a champion, and reinforce by measuring adoption and sharing it back. A skeptic who becomes a champion persuades better than any mandate.`},
        {t:`Accept paper post-op orders for thirty days, then switch them off on a set date.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The thirty days pass quietly. On day thirty-one both surgeons are still on paper, the order set is still thirty-four clicks, and now there's a deadline to fight about.`,
          why:`A transition period can help, but it doesn't fix the barrier. Without fixing the order set and reinforcing the change, a deadline just postpones the fight.`},
        {t:`Have the chief medical officer tell the surgeons in writing that paper ends today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The letter goes out at noon. By evening the two surgeons are giving verbal orders to nurses instead, which is worse than paper, and three others have stopped speaking to Walt.`,
          why:`A mandate that ignores what people are struggling with pushes the work somewhere riskier. Fix the barrier and use the champion first, and save escalation for later.`},
        {t:`Let Pat keep typing in the paper orders, since they still end up in the system.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`Pat keeps typing. The surgeons never see an allergy alert or a dose check, and the CPOE rate in the board report stays where it was a year ago.`,
          why:`Transcription isn't CPOE. The prescriber has to see the decision support while the order can still change. Accepting the workaround gives up the safety the system was built for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s6`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L5`, title:`The clock`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[...((s.picks||[])[4]===0?[`<prop type="text" from="Walt Hagen" time="9:52 PM">CPOE 88. Post op set 12 clicks. A third surgeon asked for a vest. Told him to get his own. W.</prop>`,`It was the first text Walt had ever sent her. Renee said it might be the first he'd sent anyone.`]:[]),
        `Wednesday, October 20, at 2:15 AM, the Laramie pharmacy queue stopped receiving new orders. Kiran opened a severity one call with Corvane at 2:19, put pharmacy on its downtime procedure, phone and paper, and logged every minute. Corvane's line answered with a recording and an automated email. An engineer called back at 5:09.`,
        `Corvane's support agreement said a person would respond to a severity one call within thirty minutes, at any hour. Kiran's log said two hours and fifty minutes, and forty-two orders verified by phone in the meantime.`,
        `Thursday at eight, before Corvane's incident report came out, Brett Talbot called. <span class="said" data-who="Brett" data-tone="Plain, serious, uncomfortable.">"You'll get our report at noon. I'd rather you heard this from me first. Our system starts and stops the clock on that automated email, so the monthly report is going to say we met the SLA. We didn't. Our night engineer for this region was covering two other go-lives, and nobody backed him up. That's on us."</span> He stopped. <span class="said" data-who="Brett">"My boss doesn't know I'm telling you how the clock works."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use Corvane's monthly report as the record, since vendors track their own SLAs.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The report says met. Nobody at Cascade can show otherwise without Kiran's log, and Kiran's log goes in a drawer.`,
          why:`A vendor's own report measures what its system counts. Measure SLAs with your own data, so you can tell met from reported as met.`},
        {t:`Tell Ochoa that Corvane can't support Wyoming, and start looking at other vendors.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:0},
          after:`Ochoa asks how many severity ones Corvane has missed. One, in a week of go-live. He asks what the contract says to do about it.`,
          why:`One miss calls for the contract's remedies and a fix, not a new vendor. Escalate when misses repeat, as the contract's exit terms allow.`},
        {t:`Measure it from Kiran's log, claim the contract's credit, and require a staffing fix.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria answers Brett before anyone else. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Thank you. I mean that."</span> Then she does the rest in writing. Response is measured from Cascade's log, from the call to a person working the problem: 170 minutes against 30. The contract gives a service credit for a missed severity one, and Pam claims it. The November vendor review gets a standing line: response times by severity, measured by both sides, with any difference explained. Corvane adds a second night engineer for the region through December. Pam adds a line to the contract file: response means a person, not an email. Corvane's report arrives at noon and says met. Pam staples Kiran's log to it.`,
          why:`Evaluate a vendor against its SLA with your own measurements and the contract's definitions. Use the remedies the contract provides, require a fix to the cause, and review service levels at a regular vendor meeting. An honest account manager is worth thanking, and the contract still applies.`},
        {t:`Thank Brett, ask Corvane for a root cause, and leave the credit, since he was honest.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Corvane sends a good root cause by Monday. The credit goes unclaimed, the report still says met, and the next miss, in December, gets measured on Corvane's clock too.`,
          why:`Thanking him is right, and so is the root cause. Waiving the remedy teaches both sides the SLA is optional, and it leaves the clock counting the wrong thing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s7`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L14`, title:`Ninety per hundred`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, October 22, the end of the first week, Tasha sent the chart before the morning huddle.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Help desk tickets per day, first week of hypercare, all three hospitals" labels="Sat,Sun,Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri" values="212,148,171,109,82,61,41">Tickets logged each day from Saturday, October 16, through Friday, October 22. Eight hundred twenty-four in all.</prop>`,
        `Under it she'd written what the chart couldn't show. For its size, Rawlins had by far the most tickets of the three hospitals, and the lowest share fixed on the first call. Most came from nights, and most of those said some version of the same thing: how do I move a patient to a swing bed.`,
        `Gail called after the huddle. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"My managers want to know if I should bring in agency nurses who already know Corvane,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Or send everybody back to class. Or wait it out. You've done this before. Tell me what to do."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pick the fix for her, a swing bed session on nights, and have Kiran schedule it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The session is good, and Gail is grateful. It's also the second time this year Fort Collins has decided something for Rawlins without asking, and her managers notice.`,
          why:`Your recommendation is probably right. Making the call for her skips the owner's decision and what she knows about her own staff. Recommend, then let her choose.`},
        {t:`Tell Gail it's her hospital, and she should make whatever staffing call she thinks best.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Gail goes with the agency nurses, because nobody showed her the numbers. They know Corvane and not swing beds, and the tickets keep coming.`,
          why:`Leaving her alone with the decision isn't respect. She asked for help, and you had the data that would make her choice a good one.`},
        {t:`Approve the agency nurses she mentioned, since they already know Corvane well.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Three agency nurses arrive Tuesday, at a rate that would have paid for a month of superusers. They file swing bed tickets too.`,
          why:`Buying help before reading the trend spends money on the wrong problem. The tickets pointed to one workflow, not a shortage of nurses.`},
        {t:`Bring her the data and three options with their costs, recommend one, and let her pick.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria and Tasha bring her one page on Saturday morning. Rawlins: 180 tickets from 200 users, 90 per 100 users, against Cheyenne's 50. First-contact resolution 55 percent, because the desk couldn't answer swing bed questions any better than the nurses could. Three options. Agency nurses: expensive, and they'd know Corvane but not Rawlins. Class for everyone: twelve hours of nurses off the floor in the hardest week. Or a targeted fix: Kiran's analyst simplifies the swing bed screen, a superuser runs a ten-minute huddle on it at every night shift change, and the desk gets a swing bed script so it can answer on the first call. Maria recommends the third and says why. Gail reads to the bottom. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"The third one. And I'm keeping the page."</span> A week later Rawlins is at 38 tickets per 100 users, and the desk fixes 81 percent of its calls on the first try.`,
          why:`Act as a consultant to the business. Understand the need behind the request, bring the data, lay out options with their costs, recommend one, and let the owner decide. Here the ticket trend pointed to one workflow, so the fix could be targeted.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s8`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C3`, title:`Page two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, October 23, at seven in the evening, Kiran took the marker from her at the command center whiteboard. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"You have a date,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Go. I've got it."</span>`,
        `She picked Lucia up outside her apartment in Greeley at 8:15. Lucia got in with her backpack still on, the way she had since kindergarten. Maria didn't drive to a restaurant. She drove to the hospital, up the parking garage ramp to the third level, and pulled into the spot by the stairwell. From there you could see the fourth floor. The ICU's lights were on, the way they always were, all night.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Quiet, curious.">"Is this where you parked?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Fourteen years."</span> Maria turned off the engine. In May she'd sat in Irene's driveway for six minutes before she could go in. This time she didn't wait.`,
        `So she told it. Her first year. A man sent down from a little hospital in Wyoming in the middle of the night, straight up to the ICU, no stop in the ER. A clot in his lung, and orders for a heparin drip. He was on high-flow oxygen and too short of breath to give anyone more than his name. Irene at the charge desk, pulling his medication list off the fax machine and putting it in Maria's hand. One page. It ended near the bottom, and it looked finished. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, steady, low.">"There was a page two,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, steady, low.">"It never came through. His blood thinner was on it, warfarin. He took it every night with supper, and his doctor had raised the dose that week."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, slow.">"I had the heparin hung and the pump set. His baseline labs were drawn and not back yet, and the protocol let the bolus go first. I had it drawn up in my hand."</span> Her right hand came up a little off the steering wheel, as if the syringe were still in it. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, slow.">"The phone at the desk rang. Twenty to three in the morning. It was his daughter, calling from Wyoming. She couldn't sleep. She wanted to know if we knew her dad was on a blood thinner, because nobody had asked her."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I put the syringe down. I called the resident, and we got the lab to run his INR first. It came back at five point two. If I'd pushed that bolus, he could have bled into his brain, and nobody would have known why until somebody found page two."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Very quiet.">"Did he live?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"He lived. He went home eleven days later. His daughter drove him. I never met her."</span> Maria looked up at the windows. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Bare, honest, unsteady.">"That's the luck. Not me. A woman a hundred and fifty miles away who couldn't sleep. Nobody counted the pages. Nothing caught it. Somebody's daughter called."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"I never told anybody. I thought it was mine. Then in May, at Irene's kitchen table, she told me she'd handed me that fax without looking for a cover sheet, and she'd carried it all these years too. That page you found in her binder. Count the pages. She wrote it after. She called it ours."</span>`,
        `Lucia had a lot of questions, and she started with the big one. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Soft, careful, a little hurt.">"Okay, but why not before? I asked you three times. Once for my essay. Once at the fridge with my matrix. Once over Irene's binder. You kept saying not tonight like that was an answer."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Slow, honest, close to breaking.">"Because you're going to work up there,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Slow, honest, close to breaking.">"Fourth floor, that desk. You'll be a new grad at that desk, and somebody's going to hand you a piece of paper at three in the morning. I didn't want you walking on with my story in your pocket, scared of every page. And I didn't want the first thing you knew about your mother as a nurse to be that she got lucky."</span>`,
        `Lucia undid her seat belt so she could turn and look at her straight on, backpack and all. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Firm, gentle.">"Mom, you put the syringe down."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"The phone rang."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Firm, gentle.">"Yeah, and half my cohort would've told the daughter they'd call her back once the drip was running. Honestly I might have. You didn't do that."</span> Maria put her right hand down in her lap. It had been up off the wheel since she got to the syringe. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, open.">"Does it scare you? Knowing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Some. I'll count the pages. What was his name?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, firm.">"I can't tell you. Privacy doesn't run out after eighteen years. I do still remember it."</span>`,
        `Lucia's next question was a nurse's question more than a daughter's, and Maria liked her for it. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Quiet, direct.">"So is page two fixed? For real patients, I mean, not in your test."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Between our hospitals there's nothing to send anymore. The man from Rawlins on Monday, Greeley just opened his chart."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"And from outside? Casper, Denver, wherever."</span> She had a point. Patients from other hospitals still came in through Tributary, one list at a time. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"How do you know those come in whole?"</span>`,
        ...((s.picks||[])[2]===3?[`<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's on my whiteboard. Every day."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"And when the whiteboard comes down?"</span> Maria didn't have an answer for that one yet.`]:[])],
      opts:[
        {t:`Count every outside transfer: did the full list arrive, and was it reconciled before a dose.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Maria gets out her phone and writes it to Kiran while Lucia watches, because she'd rather do it tonight than mean to. Every transfer into a Cascade hospital from outside: the count the sender says it sent, the count that arrived, and whether reconciliation was done before the first dose. Weekly, by hospital, owned by the pharmacy director, who calls on every miss. Lucia reads over her shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Send me the number too."</span> So on Monday Maria does, and every Monday after. Outside transfers 37. Complete 37. Reconciled before first dose 35. Pharmacy calls about the two that day. Lucia answers each Monday's number with a thumbs up, and once, in November, with a photo of a plastic pill bottle from skills lab and two words. Counted it.`,
          why:`A clinical metric needs a numerator and a denominator that match the safety goal. Here that's outside transfers whose whole list arrived, and reconciliation done before the first dose, counted on every transfer, with an owner who acts on each miss.`},
        {t:`Count how often the short-list banner fires on a transfer, and report it every week.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The banner fires on two lists the first week, both times correctly. The count can't say whether the nurses read it, or whether every other list arrived whole.`,
          why:`The banner count is a good sign the check works. It doesn't measure whether every list arrived complete, or whether reconciliation happened before a dose.`},
        {t:`Point to September's test results, when every one of the edge scripts finally passed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Lucia looks at her. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Your test didn't have anybody in the bed."</span> She's right, and they both know it.`,
          why:`Testing proves the build. After go-live, measure the real process on real patients, because practice, partners, and data keep changing.`},
        {t:`Ask receiving nurses in the thirty-day survey if outside transfer lists look complete.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The survey asks in November. The answers are friendly and vague. A list can look complete and be short. That was the whole problem.`,
          why:`Perceptions matter, but a short list can look finished. Measure the medication counts and the reconciliation directly.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s9`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Minutes in the chart`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, November 16, Aisha forwarded a question from Glenn with one of her own on top. Glenn wanted to know whether One Chart was costing nurses time. Aisha wanted to know whether she could tell the council it wasn't.`,
        `Kiran had pulled nursing documentation time per twelve-hour shift at Laramie from the system's own audit data: the median of MedaLink's last twelve weeks, then Corvane's first four.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Laramie nursing documentation minutes per 12-hour shift" labels="Baseline median,Week 1,Week 2,Week 3,Week 4" values="142,171,163,154,148">The median of twelve weeks on MedaLink before go-live, then each of the first four weeks on Corvane.</prop>`,
        `Glenn's analyst had already done the math the simple way. Week one was twenty-nine minutes a shift worse than MedaLink, across three hundred nurses, and he'd underlined the total.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report week one's twenty-nine extra minutes alone, since that's what nurses lived through.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Glenn's number goes to the finance committee. The board hears that One Chart costs nurses half an hour a shift, a week before anyone sees the curve coming down.`,
          why:`Week one is real, and reporting it alone mistakes a learning curve for a lasting cost. Show the trend.`},
        {t:`Plot each week against the baseline median, call it a learning curve, and keep watching.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:3},
          after:`Maria sends them both the chart with the baseline median drawn across it. Four points, all above 142, all falling: 171, 163, 154, 148. It's a learning curve, it isn't over, and she says both things. Kiran adds Cheyenne, which is falling faster, and the Corvane hospitals from their own go-live years ago, which flattened near baseline by week ten. She asks for four more weeks before anyone calls it a cost or a win. With it goes the thirty-day survey. Nurses rate the system 3.1 out of 5, against MedaLink's 2.6 in the spring, and the top complaint is the swing bed screen, already fixed. Walt's comment says only: Discharge, 39 clicks. Measured. Aisha takes it to the council as written. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Promising,"</span> she tells them, and catches Maria's eye. She learned that word from Maria in March.`,
          why:`Interpret outcomes over time against a baseline. Right after go-live, measures like documentation time often rise and then fall as people learn. Plot every point, compare like with like, pair it with user satisfaction against its own baseline, and don't call it early either way.`},
        {t:`Average the four weeks, and give the council and Glenn one number to work with.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`The average, 159, describes no week that actually happened. It hides the slope, which was the only thing worth knowing.`,
          why:`Averaging a trend erases it. Show each point against the baseline.`},
        {t:`Report week four on its own, six minutes over baseline, since week one was learning.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Six minutes sounds fine, and it's one week. In week six a Laramie unit runs short-staffed and documentation time jumps, and nobody has a trend to tell noise from a problem.`,
          why:`Week one is part of the story. Showing a single point hides the trend, and one week can't tell signal from noise.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s10`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E5`, title:`Accounts`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, December 7, Gail sent the portal numbers herself, with a subject line in capitals. CHRISTMAS CAME EARLY.`,
        `Since go-live, nurses and unit clerks had signed patients up before discharge, on the patient's phone or a caregiver's, the way they'd planned in July. Sixty-one percent of Rawlins inpatients had gone home with an account, against fourteen percent on MedaLink's portal. Gail wanted to tell her board.`,
        `Kiran had pulled one more number, and he sent it to Maria alone. Of the patients who'd signed up since October 16, twenty-two percent had logged in again after they got home.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Send Gail's board the sixty-one percent now, and add the other measures next year.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The board applauds sixty-one percent. In the spring someone asks how many patients actually use it, and the answer is the same twenty-two.`,
          why:`Sign-ups measure enrollment, not benefit. A portal improves outcomes only if patients use it, so report use and work on it.`},
        {t:`Report the sign-ups with a footnote saying repeat logins are still low.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The footnote is honest, and nobody reads it. Gail's board hears sixty-one percent and moves on, and the patients who never came back stay away.`,
          why:`Being honest about use is right. Reporting it in a footnote without acting on it leaves the gap where it is.`},
        {t:`Report active use beside sign-ups, and find out why most patients never come back.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Maria calls Gail with both numbers before Gail's board meets. Gail is unhappy for about a minute. Then she calls twelve patients herself. Most never got a notice when a result posted, because notices went to email by default, and half of Rawlins doesn't check email. Several live past the cattle guard and didn't know results could come by phone. Kiran's team switches the default to text, Rawlins nurses show each patient one result on their own phone before discharge, and the heart failure nurse in Cheyenne asks her patients to send one weight through the portal before their first follow-up call. By January forty-seven percent come back. Gail's board hears both numbers, and one more: nine heart failure patients sent weights in, and two got a medication change by phone instead of a trip to the ED.`,
          why:`Patient-facing technology improves outcomes only when patients use it. Measure activation and use, not accounts. Find the barriers, like notification defaults or poor signal, and fix them. Tie the measure to an outcome, like weights sent in by heart failure patients.`},
        {t:`Mail every new account holder a letter reminding them the portal is there.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The letters go out in January. Logins tick up for a week, the way they do after any letter, and settle back.`,
          why:`Reminders help a little, and briefly. Find out why patients don't come back, and fix that.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s11`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC5`, title:`Line 141`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, December 9, Corvane's quarterly upgrade was set for Saturday night, December 18, at every Cascade hospital, the first since Wyoming joined. The release notes ran 214 pages and listed 1,906 changes.`,
        `Kiran's junior analyst had read all 214 pages, which nobody had asked her to do, and flagged one line on page 141. The part of Corvane that read outside documents would now follow next-page links by a new default, set site by site. Corvane's note said no action was needed for most customers.`,
        `Corvane's upgrade lead wanted to keep the date. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Seven hundred customers run this version already,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Test what you like. But if the eighteenth slips, it waits for March."</span>`,
        ...(G.skippedregression?[`Maria thought about the discharge lists that had printed short on go-live morning, from scripts nobody had rerun. She didn't say it out loud. Kiran did.`]:[])],
      opts:[
        {t:`Apply it on the eighteenth as planned, since seven hundred customers run this version.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`It goes in Saturday night. By Tuesday, outside transfers are arriving twenty medications at a time again. The banner catches most of them. Kiran finds the setting on Wednesday.`,
          why:`Other customers' experience isn't your configuration. Test upgrades in a copy of your own production before they reach patients.`},
        {t:`Test it in a copy of production, with full regression and the edge scripts first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:6},
          after:`The upgrade goes into a copy of production on Monday. The junior analyst runs all 140 regression scripts, the edge scripts first: zero, nineteen, twenty, twenty-one, twenty-three, forty. Everything past twenty fails. The new default is set site by site, and Cascade's three newest sites came in with it switched off. On the twenty-three medication transfer, twenty arrive. She logs it as severity one, and Corvane fixes the setting in the package by Wednesday. Thursday she reruns everything, and it all passes. The upgrade goes in Saturday at ten, with every unit on its downtime view for ninety minutes, a back-out plan nobody needs, and a one-page note to every nurse about the three changes they'll see. Kiran forwards Maria the defect without a word. The subject line says page two.`,
          why:`Maintaining a system means keeping it current, safely. Read the release notes against your build, test the upgrade in a non-production copy with full regression and the edge cases, schedule it with downtime procedures and a back-out plan, and tell users what changes.`},
        {t:`Test only the 37 changes the notes say touch Cascade's build, then go on the 18th.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The 37 changes pass. Line 141 wasn't one of them, because the note said no action was needed. The Tuesday after, a patient from Casper reaches Cheyenne with twenty of her twenty-three medications, the banner says so, and a pharmacist spends an hour on the phone getting the rest.`,
          why:`Targeting tests from the release notes is smart. A note that says no action needed can still hit your configuration, so regression on critical workflows runs every time.`},
        {t:`Skip this upgrade until hypercare is completely over, and take the next one in March.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`March brings two quarters of changes in one package, including security fixes Nate wanted in December. The upgrade after that is bigger still.`,
          why:`Falling behind on upgrades piles up changes and delays security fixes. Stay current, and test well.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s12`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SD4`, title:`Three months`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 4, at 7:30, the three-month benefits review was the only item on Ochoa's agenda. The plan the board approved in April set the first formal review at six months, but Glenn had asked for an early read, and Glenn didn't ask for things he didn't mean to use.`,
        `Each owner had sent a page. Gail and the other two CNOs sent scan compliance. The chief medical officers at Greeley and Fort Collins sent repeat workups on Wyoming transfers. The revenue cycle director sent denials, with a note that three months of claims weren't enough to say anything yet. Glenn's team sent the MedaLink invoices, the last one paid in November. Walt sent one line. 39.`,
        `Maria's draft summary had one sentence at the top. Rawlins scan compliance up from 62 percent to 95. It was true, and it was the sentence the board would remember.`,
        (G.softroi
          ?`Glenn had read it first. He'd brought the first draft of the business case with him, the one with Brett's spreadsheet in it, and he set it on the table without opening it.`
          :`Glenn had read it first, with his pencil.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lead with Rawlins rising from 62 to 95 percent, the strongest number in the review.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-4,health:-1},
          after:`The board hears 62 to 95 and gives One Chart all of it. In April Glenn's analyst asks how much came from telepharmacy, and the answer costs Maria the room.`,
          why:`Leading with the biggest number credits the project with gains other changes made. Measure from the baseline in the plan, and give other work its credit.`},
        {t:`Have clinical systems write up every measure against its baseline, for the board to read.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's thorough and accurate. The board reads it as IT grading its own project, and the CNOs, who'll own these numbers for years, hear their own results second.`,
          why:`Measuring against the baseline is right. The benefits belong to the business owners, and they should report them. IT measures and supports.`},
        {t:`Wait for the six-month review, since three months is too early to measure anything.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Glenn gets his early read from somebody else's spreadsheet. It's wrong in two places, and it reaches the finance committee before her review does.`,
          why:`Early measures, honestly labeled, catch problems while they're fixable. Three months can show trends, even when some benefits need longer.`},
        {t:`Show each owner's measure against the plan's baseline, and say what isn't in yet.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria rewrites the top. Rawlins scan compliance was 82 percent before go-live. That's the baseline the plan set once telepharmacy and the noon list had held. Kiran pulled it from the eMAR for May through July, all shifts, counting every dose the way he had for February's restated report. December was 95. That's 13 points, and One Chart claims only that. The climb from 62 belongs to Cheyenne's night pharmacists and Gail's charge nurses, and the page names them. Laramie went from 81 to 94. MedaLink costs are gone on schedule. Repeat workups on Wyoming transfers are down, too soon to call. Denials: not yet. Each line is signed by its owner, Walt's included. Glenn reads to the bottom and sets down his pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Dry, quiet, approving.">"I'll take this one to the committee as written."</span>`,
          why:`Benefits realization measures each benefit against the baseline set before go-live, reported by the business owner who delivers it, at the review points in the plan. Credit other changes their share, and say plainly which benefits can't be measured yet.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f11s7`, title:`Tickets per 100 users, and first-call fixes`, setup:[`The first week of hypercare, Saturday, October 16, through Friday, October 22. Users are the people with a login at each hospital. The table counts each hospital's tickets and how many the service desk resolved on the first contact.`,`Tickets per 100 users = tickets / users x 100. First-contact resolution = tickets resolved on first contact / all tickets. Round to two decimals.`],
      table:[[`Hospital`,`Users`,`Tickets`,`Fixed 1st`],[`Cheyenne`,`760`,`380`,`266`],[`Laramie`,`440`,`264`,`198`],[`Rawlins`,`200`,`180`,`99`]],
      fields:[{label:`Cheyenne tickets per 100 users`, answer:50, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins tickets per 100 users`, answer:90, tol:0.01},{label:`All three, tickets per 100 users`, answer:58.86, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins first-contact resolution, percent`, answer:55, tol:0.01},{label:`All three, first-contact resolution, percent`, answer:68.33, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Cheyenne: 380 / 760 = 0.50. Times 100 = 50 tickets per 100 users.`,`Laramie: 264 / 440 = 0.60, or 60. Rawlins: 180 / 200 = 0.90, or 90.`,`All three: add the counts, never the rates. (380 + 264 + 180) / (760 + 440 + 200) = 824 / 1,400 = 0.58857. Times 100 = 58.86 tickets per 100 users.`,`Rawlins first-contact resolution: 99 / 180 = 0.55, or 55.00 percent. Cheyenne: 266 / 380 = 0.70. Laramie: 198 / 264 = 0.75.`,`All three: (266 + 198 + 99) / 824 = 563 / 824 = 0.68325, or 68.33 percent.`,`Rawlins had the fewest tickets and the heaviest load per person, and the lowest first-call fix rate. Raw counts would have pointed at Cheyenne. That's why the rate goes on the board.`] },
    { after:`f11s12`, title:`Percent change from baseline`, setup:[`December, the third month on One Chart. Rawlins gave 4,700 doses, and 4,465 had both the wristband and the medication scanned. Laramie gave 17,500 doses, and 16,450 were scanned.`,`The benefits plan's baselines, counted from the eMAR the same way as February's restated report: Rawlins 82 percent, measured May through July once telepharmacy and the noon list had held, and Laramie 81 percent. Rawlins was at 62 percent before telepharmacy started.`,`BCMA scan compliance = scanned doses / doses given. Percent change = (new minus baseline) / baseline. Round percents to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Rawlins December scan compliance, percent`, answer:95, tol:0.01},{label:`Laramie December scan compliance, percent`, answer:94, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins gain, percentage points`, answer:13, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins percent change from its 82 percent baseline`, answer:15.85, tol:0.01},{label:`Laramie percent change from its 81 percent baseline`, answer:16.05, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins percent change if measured from 62 instead`, answer:53.23, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Rawlins: 4,465 / 4,700 = 0.95, or 95.00 percent.`,`Laramie: 16,450 / 17,500 = 0.94, or 94.00 percent.`,`Rawlins gain in points: 95 minus 82 = 13 percentage points.`,`Rawlins percent change: 13 / 82 = 0.15854, or 15.85 percent.`,`Laramie: (94 minus 81) / 81 = 13 / 81 = 0.16049, or 16.05 percent.`,`From 62: (95 minus 62) / 62 = 33 / 62 = 0.53226, or 53.23 percent. That's the number that would have gone on the board's first slide, and most of it belongs to telepharmacy and the noon list. One Chart's honest claim is 13 points, 15.85 percent.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f11s2`, title:`Pick the training method`, intro:`Four methods come up on every go-live and on the exam. Pick the one each situation calls for.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Twelve traveling ED doctors arrive on different weeks and need the basics before their first shift.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:0, why:`Self-paced e-learning reaches people whose schedules won't fit a class. Pair it with a proficiency check before access.`},
      {prompt:`Pharmacists must learn a complex new verification workflow with many decision points, and want to practice on test patients with an instructor.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:1, why:`Instructor-led classroom training with hands-on practice fits complex workflows and lets learners ask questions.`},
      {prompt:`Twelve Wyoming nurses get deep training in August so they can teach and support their own units after the vendor leaves.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:2, why:`Train the trainer prepares a small group to teach their peers, which scales and keeps the skill in the building.`},
      {prompt:`At 3 AM a Rawlins nurse can't find the swing bed transfer screen, and a superuser shows her at the bedside.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:3, why:`A superuser beside the clinician in the moment of work is at-the-elbow support.`},
      {prompt:`Five hundred staff need a short refresher on a changed sign-in screen before the December upgrade.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:0, why:`A short, simple change for many people is a good fit for self-paced e-learning.`},
      {prompt:`After two days of extra training, a surgeon teaches the new order sets to the rest of his department.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:2, why:`A trained peer teaching colleagues is train the trainer. Peer teachers often carry more weight than outside instructors.`},
      {prompt:`New unit clerks learn registration and ADT workflows in a training room, with an instructor walking them through realistic scenarios.`, choices:[`Computer-based learning`,`Classroom`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`], answer:1, why:`Instructor-led training with practice in a training environment is classroom training.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`During go-live, trained superusers work beside clinicians on the units and help them at the moment of work. Which training method is this?`, opts:[`Computer-based learning`,`Train the trainer`,`At-the-elbow support`,`Classroom instruction`], a:2, why:`At-the-elbow support puts superusers on the floor beside clinicians during go-live. Computer-based learning is self-paced e-learning, classroom training is led by an instructor, and train the trainer prepares a few people deeply so they can teach their peers.`, task:`SC3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Twelve traveling physicians will start on different weeks after go-live, and none can attend the scheduled class. Which plan is BEST?`, opts:[`A two-page tip sheet and a login on arrival`,`Role-based e-learning and a proficiency check`,`The full classroom course before any shift`,`Access now, with training after week one`], a:1, why:`Match the method to the learner. Role-based computer-based learning fits schedules a class can't, and a proficiency check before access proves the skill. A tip sheet isn't training, a class nobody can attend trains nobody, and access before training puts patients at risk.`, task:`L15`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Six hundred nurses at three hospitals need EHR training before go-live, and there are six trainers. Which plan scales BEST?`, opts:[`Train superusers to teach and support peers`,`Have the six trainers run every class themselves`,`Assign e-learning modules alone, with no practice`,`Bring in vendor trainers for the go-live day only`], a:0, why:`Train the trainer multiplies a small team. Trainers teach unit superusers, who teach their peers and then stay at the elbow at go-live. Six trainers alone can't reach six hundred nurses in time, e-learning without practice doesn't build skill, and day-one vendor trainers arrive after the learning should have happened.`, task:`SC3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The service desk logged 400 tickets in a week and resolved 300 of them on the first contact. Its SLA sets a 70 percent target. What is its first-contact resolution rate?`, opts:[`75 percent`,`25 percent`,`133 percent`,`33 percent`], a:0, why:`First-contact resolution = tickets resolved on first contact / all tickets = 300 / 400 = 0.75, or 75 percent. 25 percent is the share that needed another contact, 133 percent divides the wrong way, and 33 percent compares the 100 tickets not resolved on first contact with the 300 that were. First-contact resolution is a performance indicator, so compare it with the 70 percent target: 75 percent meets it.`, task:`L5`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`BCMA scan compliance was 80 percent before go-live and 92 percent at the three-month review. What is the percent change from baseline?`, opts:[`12 percent`,`13 percent`,`92 percent`,`15 percent`], a:3, why:`Percent change = (new minus baseline) / baseline = (92 minus 80) / 80 = 12 / 80 = 0.15, or 15 percent. Twelve is the change in percentage points, and 13 percent divides by the new value instead of the baseline.`, task:`SD4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A unit's scan compliance rose from 62 to 82 percent after telepharmacy started and held there, then reached 95 percent after the EHR go-live. What gain should the EHR's benefits review claim?`, opts:[`33 points, from 62 to 95`,`13 points, from 82 to 95`,`20 points, from 62 to 82`,`None, since it was rising`], a:1, why:`The EHR claims only the gap left after other changes, measured from the baseline set before go-live once the earlier gain held. Starting from 62 takes telepharmacy's 20 points. Thirteen points from 82 is a 15.85 percent change: 13 / 82.`, task:`SD4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A vendor's monthly report marks an SLA as met. The hospital's own log shows a severity one call waited 170 minutes for a person, against a 30-minute target. What should the IT leader do?`, opts:[`Accept the vendor's report as the record`,`Waive the credit to protect the relationship`,`Begin replacing the vendor right away`,`Use its own log and the contract's remedies`], a:3, why:`Evaluate SLAs with your own measurements and the contract's definitions, use the remedies the contract gives you, and require a fix to the cause. One miss calls for remedies and review, not replacement, and waiving the credit teaches both sides the SLA is optional.`, task:`L5`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Two surgeons go back to paper orders after go-live, saying the post-op order set takes too many clicks. What is the BEST first response?`, opts:[`Have leadership ban paper orders today`,`Fix the order set, with a peer champion`,`Let the unit clerk keep entering them`,`Allow paper for 30 days, then stop it`], a:1, why:`Change management treats resistance as information. Fix the real barrier, put a respected peer at the elbow as a champion, and reinforce by measuring adoption. A ban pushes work into verbal orders, clerk entry isn't CPOE, and a deadline alone just delays the fight.`, task:`L20`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A nurse executive asks the IT leader to tell her how to handle a spike in support tickets on her units. Which response BEST reflects consultative services?`, opts:[`Decide for her, then schedule the fix`,`Say it's her unit and her decision`,`Bring data and options, recommend one`,`Approve the agency staff she named`], a:2, why:`Consultative services diagnose the need, bring data and options with their costs, recommend one, and leave the decision with the owner. Deciding for her skips the owner, and saying it's hers alone withholds the analysis she asked for.`, task:`L14`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Thirty days after go-live, nurses rate the new EHR 3.1 out of 5. What makes this score MOST useful?`, opts:[`A comparison with the old system's score`,`A comparison with the vendor's brochure`,`An average with the physicians' scores`,`A rule to report it only above 4 of 5`], a:0, why:`User satisfaction means the most against a baseline, like the old system's score before go-live, along with what changed because of the comments. Vendor claims and blended averages don't measure your users, and hiding low scores hides the problem.`, task:`L6`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`An EHR upgrade needs two hours of planned downtime. Which plan is BEST?`, opts:[`Run it at 2 AM on a weeknight, with no notice to staff`,`Plan it with clinical leaders, test, warn staff`,`Save it for one yearly window with all other upgrades`,`Let the vendor pick the time that suits its engineers`], a:1, why:`Plan maintenance downtime with the people it affects: a low census time chosen with clinical leaders, the upgrade tested in a copy first, downtime procedures and read-only views ready, notice well ahead, and a back-out plan. 2 AM with no warning still hits the night shift, one yearly window delays fixes, and the vendor doesn't run your units.`, task:`SC5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which measure BEST shows whether medication lists from outside hospitals arrive complete?`, opts:[`Transfers whose full list arrived`,`Times the short-list banner fired`,`Nurse ratings of list quality`,`Scripts passed before go-live`], a:0, why:`A clinical metric needs a numerator and denominator that match the goal: transfers whose complete list arrived, out of all transfers. The banner count shows the check works, ratings are perceptions, and test results describe the build, not real patients.`, task:`C3`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Sixty-one percent of discharged patients leave with a portal account, but 22 percent log in again at home. What should the hospital track and improve?`, opts:[`The share of patients with accounts`,`Letters mailed to account holders`,`Active use, like results viewed`,`Sign-ups taken at the front desk`], a:2, why:`Patient-facing technology improves outcomes only when patients use it. Track activation and use, find the barriers, like notification defaults or poor signal, and fix them. Accounts and letters measure effort, not benefit.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`After an EHR go-live, an outside hospital stops receiving transfer documents through the regional HIE. What is the BEST response?`, opts:[`Fix that one hospital's connection`,`Fax all summaries until it's fixed`,`Have outside nurses phone for records`,`Find, notify, and verify every partner`], a:3, why:`A go-live changes data flows outside partners depend on. Find every receiving organization, notify them, and verify they receive, with a safe fallback in the meantime. Fixing one leaves the others broken, and fax and phone calls bring back the risks the feed removed.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A vendor's quarterly upgrade is scheduled. Its release notes say no action is needed for most changes. What should the team do?`, opts:[`Apply it now, since many sites run it`,`Defer it until next quarter's release`,`Test it in a copy, with regression`,`Test only the items the notes call out`], a:2, why:`Read the notes against your build, then test the upgrade in a non-production copy with full regression on critical workflows and edge cases. Other customers aren't your configuration, deferring piles up changes and security fixes, and a no-action note can still hit your setup.`, task:`SC5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Two units weren't part of the EHR design and are uneasy six weeks before go-live. What BEST reduces their resistance?`, opts:[`Send a memo from the CNO requiring support`,`Delay go-live for those two units by months`,`Add extra training for them after go-live`,`Bring their staff into workflow testing now`], a:3, why:`Change management works through involvement. Bring the uneasy units' staff into workflow validation and testing now, explain why the change matters, and name local champions. A mandate gets compliance at best, a delay splits the organization, and training after go-live comes too late to shape what they'll use.`, task:`L20`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which of these is a consultative service from an IT leader, rather than an operational one?`, opts:[`Resetting a nurse's password`,`Patching the EHR's servers`,`Running the nightly backups`,`Advising a clinic on options`], a:3, why:`Consultative services help a department decide: understand the need, bring data and options, recommend one, and leave the choice with the owner. Password resets, patching, and backups are operational work that keeps systems running.`, task:`L14`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A health plan asks a hospital system for a daily feed of clinical data on the plan's members, for its care managers. What should the hospital confirm FIRST?`, opts:[`An agreement covering the use, limited to what's needed`,`Whether the plan will pay a fee for every record sent`,`Whether each member signed the hospital's portal terms`,`Whether the vendor can build the feed this quarter`], a:0, why:`Payers and providers are separate organizations with their own roles. Before sharing, confirm the purpose is one the Privacy Rule permits, like the plan's care management for shared members, and send only the minimum necessary. For a daily bulk feed, a written data sharing agreement that sets the purpose, scope, and safeguards is the sound way to do it, with privacy in the review. A fee, portal terms, and build capacity come after the hospital knows the sharing is allowed and scoped.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The command center closed on Friday, November 12, four weeks after it opened. On its last morning Kiran erased the whiteboard except for one corner, where the junior analyst's name sat under his, and took a picture of it first. He sent the picture to Maria with no message. She knew what it meant: somebody else could take the nights now.`,
    `On the Sunday after she told Lucia, Maria had called Irene at noon, the way she did now. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Well?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"All of it. The luck too."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Gruff, warm.">"And?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"She said I put the syringe down."</span> Irene was quiet for a second. <span class="said" data-who="Irene" data-tone="Dry, warm, satisfied.">"Smart girl. Took you eighteen years to hear it."</span>`,
    ((s.picks||[])[7]===0
      ?`Monday, January 3, at 7:02 AM, Maria sent Lucia the number, the way she had every Monday since October 25. It was New Year's week, so it was small. Outside transfers 9. Complete 9. Reconciled before first dose 9. Lucia answered in under a minute. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Counted. Hospital clinicals start next week. Med surg in Greeley. They say I'll be at the elbow of a real nurse."</span> Maria read it at a red light on Mulberry and laughed out loud, alone in the car.`
      :`Monday, January 3, at 7:02 AM, Maria sent Lucia a number for the first time, the outside transfer count Kiran and pharmacy had finally built in December, after Lucia asked a second time. It was New Year's week, so it was small. Outside transfers 9. Complete 9. Reconciled before first dose 9. Lucia answered in under a minute. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Finally. Hospital clinicals start next week. Med surg in Greeley. They say I'll be at the elbow of a real nurse."</span> Maria read it at a red light on Mulberry and laughed out loud, alone in the car.`),
    `Tuesday, after the benefits review, Ochoa kept her in his office. He waited until Glenn's footsteps were gone down the hall.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"The board wants a three-year IT strategic plan at its March meeting,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"I'd like you to write it, and present it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"That's the CIO's plan."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Even, unreadable.">"It is."</span> She waited for the rest. He turned his coffee cup a quarter turn, the way he did when he was deciding how much to say. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Why does it have to be me?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, final.">"Bring me a draft by February first."</span> He opened his legal pad to a clean page. At the top he'd already written 2032, the year he'd written in July. Under it there was nothing at all.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch11.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Next Chair`
});
